# Technical appendix: mobile vs desktop conversion (observational)

*Generated by `case_studies/03_ga360.py` from `results/case_studies/ga360.json` (commit `{{ r.git_commit_at_run }}`). Do not edit the numbers by hand.*

The one-page decision is in [03_ga360.md](03_ga360.md). This appendix holds the full evidence behind it.

## What changed versus the original analysis

1. **Standard errors.** There are {{ "%.2f" | format(r.sessions_per_visitor) }} sessions per visitor, so {{ "%.1f" | format(100 * (1 - 1 / r.sessions_per_visitor)) }}% of sessions are a visitor's second or later session in the window. Clustering by `fullVisitorId` widens the naive SE by a factor of {{ "%.2f" | format(r.se_inflation_from_clustering) }}. The original CI treated every session as independent.
2. **Confounding adjustment** with propensity scores, using only covariates fixed at session start (month, weekday, UTC time block, continent, top-10 country, returning-visitor flag and prior-visit count). In-session behaviour (pageviews, time on site, bounces) was **never pulled**: it is downstream of the device and would bias the estimate.
3. **Suspected internal traffic** is excluded in a *post-hoc* sensitivity run. The hypothesis came from the initial audit of channel-level rates, before this analysis was written, but it is still a data-driven exclusion, so the full-data estimate stays the primary one. {% set rd = r.internal_traffic.referral_direct %}One segment stands out. Channel "Referral" with the source recorded as "(direct)" has {{ "{:,}".format(rd.n_desktop) }} desktop sessions but only {{ "{:,}".format(rd.n_mobile) }} mobile ones. It converts at {{ rd.conversion_rate_desktop | pct(1) }} on desktop and holds **{{ "{:,}".format(rd.conversions_desktop) }} conversions**. In total, the excluded traffic carries {{ r.internal_traffic.share_of_desktop_conversions | pct(1) }} of all desktop conversions. The pattern fits employee or internal purchasing with an obfuscated referrer, but the sample can't confirm the origin. It also drives a large share of the raw "desktop converts better" result.
4. **Sensitivity to unmeasured confounding** via an E-value.

