## What remains unidentified

- **Purchase intent.** People may browse on phones and buy on desktops. Cross-device journeys are invisible here, because the same person on two devices has two `fullVisitorId`s (no visitor appears on both devices in this data). A mobile session that leads to a later desktop purchase counts as a mobile non-conversion. That alone can produce a gap with **zero** causal effect of the mobile experience.
- **Channel** may be partly caused by device (social apps on phones), so set B can understate the total device-associated gap. The primary set A leaves it out on purpose. The two estimates bracket the ambiguity.
- **E-value:** to explain away the adjusted association entirely, an unmeasured confounder would need a risk-ratio association of **{{ "%.2f" | format(r.e_value.e_value_point) }}** with both device and conversion beyond the measured covariates. To move the CI to include no effect, it would need **{{ "%.2f" | format(r.e_value.e_value_ci) }}**. Without suspected internal traffic these fall to {{ "%.2f" | format(r.e_value_excluding_internal.e_value_point) }} and {{ "%.2f" | format(r.e_value_excluding_internal.e_value_ci) }}. The bar is two associations at once. Purchase intent's association with conversion is plausibly far above {{ "%.0f" | format(r.e_value.e_value_point) }}, since intent is close to what conversion measures. Its association with device choice is the open question. So a large E-value here **does not** make the causal reading safe; it says the confounder would have to differ strongly between mobile and desktop users.

